<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Obshchina</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as mir, rural community</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The obshchina or mir was the peasant village community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a>, made up of serfs, later emancipated serfs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-peasant">state peasants</a> and their descendants, usually of a single village. Title to the land lay with the mir, not the individual: each household farmed its own holding, which it could not sell or bequeath without the mir's consent, and the mir could repartition the land from time to time. Its assembly, the skhod, also levied recruits, punished minor crimes and answered for its members' taxes. Slavophiles hailed it as ancient and purely Russian, while the Western school of Boris Chicherin held it a later creation for tax collection; the Stolypin reforms of 1906–1914 undermined it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/obshchina.jpg" alt="Obshchina" width="330" height="366" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sergey Korovin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KorovinS_NaMiru.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The obshchina, or mir, was a peasant village community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a>, usually settled in a single village, though a village sometimes held more than one mir and several villages were sometimes combined in one. The word derives from obshchiy, &quot;common&quot;. Communal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-tenure">land tenure</a> is said to predate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> and survived emancipation and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a>. Until serfdom was abolished in 1861, a mir could contain serfs or free peasants; where it held serfs, the lands reserved for their use were assigned to the mir by the proprietor for allocation. The emancipation withdrew the village community from the jurisdiction of the landowning nobility and gave it self-government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The assembly, the skhod, consisted of all the peasant householders and elected a village elder, the starosta, and a tax collector. Several mirs were united in a volost with an assembly of elected delegates. The mir dealt with the household rather than the individual: a peasant had a right to a holding but not to a particular one, and could not dispose of it freely. Families could not be deprived of their houses or farm implements, nor the mir of its land. Responsibility for underpaid taxes was shared in the practice called krugovaya poruka, until a decree of October 1906 abolished communal responsibility for payment.</p><p>Alliances were formed mainly through marriage and kinship, and the eldest members of households usually governed the redistribution of land. A traditional calendar guided the year, telling when to work and when to keep feast days. In 1905 repartitional tenure did not exist in the Baltic provinces, was used by a quarter of western and southwestern peasants, by two thirds of steppe peasants and by 96.9 per cent elsewhere.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>August von Haxthausen's book of 1847 made the mir a topic in political philosophy. Alexander Herzen hailed it as a germ of a future socialist society, and Aleksey Khomyakov saw in it the spiritual unity of Russian society, working out a philosophy he called sobornost. European socialists looked to it as evidence that Russian peasants had a history of socialised property. Writers on both sides of the dispute over its antiquity agreed that landlords and the state played a vital part in its development, and one view held that communal tenure tends to prevail where arable land is scarce. The Stolypin agrarian reforms of 1906 to 1914 undermined it, though they made no significant dent in the structure of peasant life.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Obshchina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
